1. Every vacuity-reader brief carries THE DISJUNCT CHECK: a disjunct of a conclusion provable from the premises or the
   instance alone is vacuous; the theorem is its other disjunct under that one's negation; no unbounded-∃ collision
   predicate, CollidesAmong over an explicit list only (10-20: 61 landed theorems bound nothing).
2. Hand checks on hbox run ONLY as `SWARM_MEM_MAX=6G LEAN_NUM_THREADS=2 swarm-build …` with a maxHeartbeats cap (one
   runaway tactic at 19 GB timed out a judge's bench control). A `decide +kernel` that "sticks" may be an earlyoom kill
   (exit 143): check before concluding.
3. Every nano brief says what it is: A DEMONSTRATION MINI CAN TAKE (Ember 10-21: nano is an exercise that gathers
   requirements, explores adjacent systems and shows where mini could be better; mini stays the production demoland).
4. PID file or nothing, with no "before a slot" clause: a lander 2 s old is a judge. No `pkill -f`, no pattern kills.
5. A re-genesis costs a judge, never a design point; price PREMISES (which landed theorems gain a hypothesis). A written
   design builds now; several due redesigns converge into one day.
6. A judge log's known-red CONTROL lines (nobin "no lake-built host"; F2) read like reds: read the real run's line first.
7. After a prune: `cv lanes --recent N --unfinished` then `cv adopt` before respawning; spawn fresh only past ~400K.
8. Removing or weakening any check is a pause item; a WASTE ceiling forbids waste, never a check (an added check may raise one, said so).
9. A CROSS-LAYER line starts at column 0 (`^CROSS-LAYER:`); an indented one is invisible to owners.py and the judge refuses on owners.
10. (Ember 10-21) A landing is NOT judged in full. scripts/commit's default is the FAST path (rebase, cheap gates, build the reached libraries, push); you run your own filtered tests BEFORE launching, every time. CONVERGENCE (standing: build, census, every row, benches) runs on a schedule or on ROOT's word; a convergence red is attributed by read-set to the commits since the last green and fixed FORWARD by the owner, or reverted. The full judge is for `!` flag days and gates/ changes only. Holds and queue tickets are gone. (ACTIVE since e568634, 2026-10-22: scripts/commit IS the fast path; `scripts/converge` on demand; the timer runs every 3 h at :17.)
11. Closure C3, precisely (gates/closure.py): a proof module exports no def or macro, BUT Prop statements (`structure … : Prop`, `inductive … → Prop`, `def P : … → Prop`) live IN proof modules; a def module exporting one is red.
12. Each worktree uses ITS OWN cargo target dir. A shared CARGO_TARGET_DIR across two worktrees mixes trees: devtools bakes CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR at build time, so repo() and the launcher binaries come from whichever tree built last (HOSTING-16's first fence run read another tree's units and went RED).
13. Memory caps per library (measured 10-21; CLARIFIED real 10-20: this governs HAND builds; a landing through scripts/commit is the judge's own build under its slot discipline and is launched regardless of bench activity): the 6G/2-thread cap suits a single file or a small library; Hosting.Proofs.Reply needs 16G (OOM-killed twice at 10G); a whole-tree Lang.Proofs build OOMs at 10G. Raise the cap for that library only, print it, never during a judge's bench window.
14. A plant's verdict is read from the WHOLE output, not a summary grep for the expected line: GATES-22's plants printed 'ND_TICKET: unbound variable' on every attempt and passed because the grep looked only for GREEN. Grep for 'error|unbound|No such file|Traceback' in every plant output as a control.
15. A wait loop sleeps the FULL deadline per turn (one tool call of up to ~570 s), never a tight poll: the census showed lanes at ~20 tool calls/hour across 8–10-hour lifetimes, nearly all deadline polls on judges; each poll is a turn of context. With GATES-22 a landing should not outlive an hour; a lane still waiting past that asks ROOT what it waits on.
16. A plant's INPUT is the real failure, captured from a box log by path and line, never a line the planter typed. GATES-20k (94d5bb6) matched `^strace: ` and its plant printed exactly that from a fake strace; the real strace names itself by argv[0] and writes after libtest's dots (`........../usr/bin/strace: ptrace(PTRACE_LISTEN,…): Input/output error`), so the fix never matched the failure it was written for and PROTOCOL-63 was falsely refused (found by GATES-12, real 10-20). A plant that only reproduces its author's model of the failure tests the model. Cite the log lines in the plant and run the real binary where it exists.
17. Run the SOURCE GATES yourself before every scripts/commit (`gates/check.py` from your worktree: closure C3, owners, guard discipline, the append-only rule); `lake env lean` and `lake build` do not run them. ACTIVITIES-1's first landing (real 10-20) was refused at the gate before any build: def modules exported Prop-typed judgments, proof modules exported defs and instances, and a `Calculus` structure packaged proofs into a Type (lives in neither kind). The nano pattern is Data (def module) + Laws : Prop (proof module), as Env/Ctx.Lawful and HeadSound.
18. Read back what a pipeline produced before you launch on it: grep the commit message and every generated file for the placeholders your pipeline uses (`__N__`, `TODO`, `XXX`) and the pipeline's own output for `sed:`/`error`. HOST-22's HOST-69 (real 10-20) launched with a message whose SIZE line read `+__N__` because a macOS `sed -i` failed silently; scripts/commit copies the message at startup and a judge is never stopped, so the slip is permanent in history (corrected in the next message and the decision). The wrapper-answers class: the pipeline answered for the message.
